{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}About extdir{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}What extdir is, who runs it, and what it deliberately is not.{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<div class="wrap page">
    <div class="page-head">
        <h1>About extdir</h1>
        <p>A community-run directory of open-source Shopware 6 extensions.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
        <h2>The question this answers</h2>
        <p>
            Is there an open-source extension that does what I need, is anyone still maintaining it,
            and does it work with the Shopware version I actually run? That last part is the hard bit,
            and it is why this exists. {{ total }} extensions are indexed, with the Shopware versions
            each one declares support for and how recently anyone touched the code.
        </p>

        <h2>Not affiliated with Shopware AG</h2>
        <p>
            This is an independent project with no connection to Shopware AG — not a partnership, not
            an endorsement, not an official anything. It is not a replacement for the Shopware Store,
            and it lists only open-source extensions. “Shopware” appears here descriptively, because
            that is the software these extensions are written for.
        </p>

        <h2>What it deliberately is not</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>No payments, no checkout, no paid listings, no advertising.</li>
            <li>No proprietary or commercial extensions.</li>
            <li>No accounts, no tracking, no cookies.</li>
            <li>No editorial placement. <a href="{{ path('ranking') }}">Ranking is algorithmic and published.</a></li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
        <p>
            Packages are discovered on Packagist by their Composer type, and metadata comes from what
            maintainers publish there — package names, descriptions, licences and, crucially, the
            <code>shopware/core</code> version constraint. Repository activity comes from the GitHub API.
        </p>
        <p>
            Compatibility is read from those declared constraints, so it reflects what the maintainer
            stated rather than a test result. That distinction is kept visible everywhere on the site:
            entries say “declares support for”, never “works with”, and open-ended constraints are
            shown as less certain than bounded ones.
        </p>

        <h2>Licences are taken seriously</h2>
        <p>
            A public repository with no licence file is not open source — it is all rights reserved,
            and nobody may redistribute it. Extensions where no licence can be detected are indexed and
            linked only, with a visible badge, and are never mirrored or rebuilt. Copyleft extensions
            are labelled as such rather than lumped in with MIT-style ones, because the obligations
            they place on a shop are genuinely different.
        </p>

        <h2>Who runs it</h2>
        <p>
            extdir is operated and maintained by <a href="https://runio.dev" rel="noopener">runio.dev</a>
            as a non-commercial community project. Full contact details are in the
            <a href="{{ path('imprint') }}">imprint</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The maintainer also publishes Shopware extensions. Those are indexed exactly like anyone
            else's, carry a visible disclosure badge, and receive no preferential treatment in ranking
            or verification — there is a test in the build that fails if vendor-specific logic ever
            enters that code.
        </p>

        <h2>The platform is open source</h2>
        <p>
            The code behind this site is public at
            <a href="https://github.com/extdir" rel="noopener">github.com/extdir</a>. That is not a
            nicety: a directory that asks you to trust its compatibility and licence data should let you
            check how that data was produced.
        </p>

        <h2>Found a mistake?</h2>
        <p>
            Entries are generated automatically and some of them will be wrong. If your extension is
            misrepresented, or you want it gone, the <a href="{{ path('takedown') }}">takedown policy</a>
            explains how — no justification required.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
